WEBVTT

00:00:04.600 --> 00:00:07.070
<v><b>Presenter:</b></v> Hello, and welcome to <i>The world of work.</i>

00:00:07.270 --> 00:00:10.378
Today, it’s all about working hours and productivity.

00:00:11.052 --> 00:00:14.198
There’ve been a lot of articles in the British press recently telling us

00:00:14.210 --> 00:00:18.208
that we’re working too many extra hours, that we should only work a four-day week,

00:00:18.258 --> 00:00:23.773
not a five-day week, that we shouldn’t be doing eight-hour days from nine to five and so on, and,

00:00:23.794 --> 00:00:28.700
indeed, suggesting that if we had shorter working hours, we might actually be more productive.

00:00:29.317 --> 00:00:33.202
But how realistic is it really to suggest that people work fewer hours?

00:00:33.752 --> 00:00:36.894
A lot of offices have fixed hours when they need to have people there,

00:00:37.256 --> 00:00:40.729
and having fewer people in the office may simply not be an option,

00:00:41.183 --> 00:00:44.481
not to mention that many employees may not want a drop in salary

00:00:44.677 --> 00:00:46.517
in exchange for a shorter working week.

00:00:47.267 --> 00:00:52.263
However, what a lot of experts appear to be suggesting is not so much working fewer hours,

00:00:52.596 --> 00:00:54.092
but organizing your day better.

00:00:55.033 --> 00:00:59.293
A recent study used a computer application to track employees’ work habits.

00:00:59.840 --> 00:01:04.736
Specifically, the application measured how much time people spent on various tasks,

00:01:04.902 --> 00:01:07.098
and compared this to their productivity levels.

00:01:08.015 --> 00:01:10.355
In the process of measuring people’s activity,

00:01:10.799 --> 00:01:15.240
they were surprised to discover that what was important was not how many hours people worked,

00:01:15.752 --> 00:01:18.371
but rather, how they organized their working day.

00:01:19.333 --> 00:01:24.250
In particular, people who were religious about taking short breaks were far more productive

00:01:24.267 --> 00:01:25.825
than those who worked longer hours.

00:01:26.425 --> 00:01:31.027
They discovered that the ideal work-to-break ratio was 52 minutes of work,

00:01:31.331 --> 00:01:33.641
followed by 18 minutes of rest.

00:01:34.425 --> 00:01:38.675
People who maintained this schedule had a unique level of focus in their work.

00:01:39.613 --> 00:01:40.530
So basically,

00:01:40.658 --> 00:01:44.914
what the study is proposing is that the most productive way of working an eight-hour day,

00:01:45.123 --> 00:01:50.450
if we round up the figures from the research, is for people to break their day into hourly intervals,

00:01:50.750 --> 00:01:53.950
followed by 15–20 minutes of rest each time.

00:01:54.633 --> 00:01:58.891
We naturally plan what we need to accomplish by the end of the day, the week, or the month,

00:01:59.233 --> 00:02:02.854
but we’re far more effective when we focus on what we can accomplish in an hour.

00:02:03.575 --> 00:02:05.554
Apart from getting you into the right rhythm,

00:02:05.854 --> 00:02:10.250
planning your day around hour-long intervals simplifies daunting tasks

00:02:10.412 --> 00:02:12.500
by breaking them into manageable chunks.

00:02:13.015 --> 00:02:15.881
If you want to follow the research recommendations to the letter,

00:02:16.198 --> 00:02:20.921
you can plan your day around 52-minute intervals if you like, but an hour works just as well.

00:02:21.670 --> 00:02:25.383
However, this strategy only works if you follow certain rules.

00:02:25.754 --> 00:02:30.829
For example, you have to respect the hour of work – you should absolutely not be,

00:02:30.979 --> 00:02:35.560
let’s say, checking social media, or making yourself a coffee during this hour.

00:02:35.910 --> 00:02:41.098
Then, equally, you need to respect the break time, and ideally do something completely different,

00:02:41.160 --> 00:02:44.785
like taking a short walk, chatting to colleagues, or reading

00:02:45.110 --> 00:02:47.016
– something that takes you away from your work.

00:02:47.703 --> 00:02:49.931
So now we’re going to talk to Martha,

00:02:50.310 --> 00:02:53.893
who works in the Human Resources department of a large publishing company…

00:02:54.264 --> 00:02:54.945
<v><b>Martha:</b></v> Hello.